gamescope: do not reject a Vulkan device that exposes no DRM nodes

A driver may implement VK_EXT_physical_device_drm and still report
hasPrimary == false and hasRender == false: that is what a GPU reached through
a proprietary kernel driver rather than DRM does. PanVK over kbase on a Tensor
phone is the case at hand - mali_kbase registers /dev/mali0 as a miscellaneous
char device and owns no DRM node at all, and its driver leaves
drm.{render,primary}_rdev unset (see the comment in its 004-physical-device-kbase
patch).

gamescope already runs a Vulkan device with no DRM fd: a driver that does not
implement the extension at all takes the branch above, warns, and leaves
m_drmRendererFd unset. A driver that implements it and reports empty fields was
rejected instead, which is the inconsistency this removes. Warn and carry on, and
only refuse when the driver names a node it then fails to open.

Nothing downstream needs the fd here: gamescope's Vulkan renderer allocates with
vkAllocateMemory and takes no wlr_allocator, and CTimeline - the one remaining
consumer through drmSyncobjFDToHandle - is only built from steamcompmgr.cpp.

Not yet device-tested: PanVK over kbase reaches gamescope's device selection on a
Pixel 8 Pro (Mali-G715 MC7), but this binary has not been built or run there.
The Adreno path is unchanged, since it reports both nodes.

--- a/src/rendervulkan.cpp
+++ b/src/rendervulkan.cpp
@@ -478,33 +478,32 @@
 		};
 		vk.GetPhysicalDeviceProperties2( physDev(), &props2 );
 
-		if ( !GetBackend()->UsesVulkanSwapchain() && !drmProps.hasPrimary ) {
-			vk_log.errorf( "physical device has no primary node" );
-			return false;
-		}
-		if ( !drmProps.hasRender ) {
-			vk_log.errorf( "physical device has no render node" );
-			return false;
-		}
+		// A driver may implement VK_EXT_physical_device_drm and still expose no nodes at
+		// all: a GPU reached through a proprietary kernel driver rather than DRM, such as
+		// Mali over kbase on a Tensor phone. Such a device still composites and allocates
+		// through Vulkan, so treat it the way a driver without the extension is treated
+		// above - warn, leave m_drmRendererFd unset, carry on. Only refuse when the driver
+		// names a node it then fails to open.
+		if ( !drmProps.hasPrimary || !drmProps.hasRender ) {
+			vk_log.warnf( "physical device exposes no DRM nodes; continuing without one" );
+		} else {
+			dev_t renderDevId = makedev( drmProps.renderMajor, drmProps.renderMinor );
+			drmDevice *drmDev = nullptr;
+			if ( drmGetDeviceFromDevId(renderDevId, 0, &drmDev) != 0 ) {
+				vk_log.errorf( "drmGetDeviceFromDevId() failed" );
+				return false;
+			}
+			assert(drmDev->available_nodes & (1 << DRM_NODE_RENDER));
+			const char *drmRenderName = drmDev->nodes[DRM_NODE_RENDER];
 
-		dev_t renderDevId = makedev( drmProps.renderMajor, drmProps.renderMinor );
-		drmDevice *drmDev = nullptr;
-		if (drmGetDeviceFromDevId(renderDevId, 0, &drmDev) != 0) {
-			vk_log.errorf( "drmGetDeviceFromDevId() failed" );
-			return false;
-		}
-		assert(drmDev->available_nodes & (1 << DRM_NODE_RENDER));
-		const char *drmRenderName = drmDev->nodes[DRM_NODE_RENDER];
-
-		m_drmRendererFd = open( drmRenderName, O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC );
-		drmFreeDevice(&drmDev);
-		if ( m_drmRendererFd < 0 ) {
-			vk_log.errorf_errno( "failed to open DRM render node" );
-			return false;
-		}
+			m_drmRendererFd = open( drmRenderName, O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC );
